/**
  @page guides-interfaces-dg Interfaces, traces, and discontinuous Galerkin
  @brief Select facet domains and assemble consistent interface terms.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-ifdg-intro Facet contributions

  Cell integrals describe volume contributions. Facet integrals express
  exterior fluxes and coupling between neighboring cells. The integration
  domain, one-sided values and normal orientation must be specified together.

  @section guides-ifdg-integrals Select the facet domain

  | Form | Domain | Use |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | `BoundaryIntegral` | Exterior facets | Neumann, Robin and weak Dirichlet terms |
  | `InterfaceIntegral` | Interior facets | Material interfaces and DG coupling |
  | `FaceIntegral` | All facets | Terms common to both domains |

  `.over(attribute, ...)` restricts a form to tagged facets. Prepare the
  facet-to-cell relation first. `BoundaryNormal` provides the exterior normal;
  `FaceNormal` follows the library's facet orientation.

  @section guides-ifdg-traces Choose a side

  A conforming scalar field has equal values on the two sides of an interior
  facet. A discontinuous field or a conforming field's gradient can have
  distinct traces. For derivative expressions,
  `Grad(field).traceOf(attribute)` selects the incident cell with that region
  label. Ambiguous derivative evaluation raises
  `UndeterminedTraceDomainException`. For a discontinuous field value, evaluate
  on a point mapped into the selected cell, as in the checked example.

  `mesh.trace({{attr1, attr2}, interfaceAttribute})` assigns a facet label from
  a pair of cell labels. This operation prepares attribute selection for
  subsequent interface forms; the selected field trace still specifies its
  incident region.

  @section guides-ifdg-jump Jump and average

  With a fixed ordering of incident cells, define
  @f[
    [\![w]\!]=w^+-w^-,\qquad
    \{w\}=\tfrac12(w^++w^-).
  @f]
  `Jump(w)` and `Average(w)` implement these operations. For a continuous
  trace, the jump is zero and the average equals the common value.
  The @ref examples-interface-traces "checked trace example" evaluates a
  piecewise constant field and verifies both selected traces and both operators.

  @section guides-ifdg-dg Symmetric interior penalty

  For scalar diffusion with coefficient one, choose an interior normal
  @f$n@f$ from the plus cell to the minus cell and use the same ordering in
  the jump. On a discontinuous trial/test space, the interior part is
  @f[
  \begin{aligned}
    a_i(u,v)={}&\sum_K\int_K\nabla u\cdot\nabla v
      -\sum_{F\in\mathcal F_i}\int_F\{\nabla u\}\cdot n[\![v]\!]\\
      &-\sum_{F\in\mathcal F_i}\int_F\{\nabla v\}\cdot n[\![u]\!]
      +\sum_{F\in\mathcal F_i}\int_F\sigma_F[\![u]\!][\![v]\!].
  \end{aligned}
  @f]
  The two consistency terms are transposes. A penalty of the form
  @f$\sigma_F=\eta p^2/h_F@f$ is typical for positive degree @f$p@f$;
  the sufficient constant depends on the element family and mesh geometry.

  Homogeneous Dirichlet data imposed weakly on @f$\Gamma_D@f$ adds
  @f[
    -\int_{\Gamma_D}(\nabla u\cdot n)v
    -\int_{\Gamma_D}(\nabla v\cdot n)u
    +\int_{\Gamma_D}\sigma_Fuv.
  @f]
  For prescribed value @f$g@f$, the right-hand side includes
  @f$-\int_{\Gamma_D}(\nabla v\cdot n)g
       +\int_{\Gamma_D}\sigma_Fgv@f$, in addition to the volume source.
  These terms complete the boundary treatment required by this method.

  The analysis of interior-penalty formulations is developed in
  @cite arnold2002unified. For suitable discontinuous spaces, regular meshes and a sufficiently large
  penalty, the symmetric Dirichlet diffusion form is coercive and yields an
  SPD matrix. Solver choice follows the assembled form and constraints.
  Conforming `P1` fields have zero interior value jumps, while `P0` has zero
  cellwise gradient. A DG discretization therefore requires a space and facet
  operators appropriate to the full chosen formulation.

  The @ref examples-pdes-dg-poisson "SIPG diffusion tutorial" implements the
  complete cell-local affine method, including weak boundary data, and checks
  a patch test and a four-level refinement study.

  @section guides-ifdg-seealso Continue

  - @ref examples-interface-traces "Evaluate actual interface traces"
  - @ref guides-connectivity "Prepare facet incidence"
  - @ref guides-solvers "Select a solver from matrix properties"
*/
